WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.545
Many Excel sheets are wide, with one column per month.

2
00:00:04.545 --> 00:00:07.167
Power BI works best with long tables.

3
00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:12.411
In this lesson, you will learn to unpivot month columns into rows, rename the

4
00:00:12.411 --> 00:00:15.782
results, and pivot them back when you need to.

5
00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:17.280
Let's reshape some data!

6
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.543
With Enter data, I build a small payroll table:

7
00:00:22.543 --> 00:00:27.743
a Name column, and one column for each month, August, September, and October, with

8
00:00:27.743 --> 00:00:29.600
a salary for each person.

9
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.935
I fill in the salaries for all three months. Notice:

10
00:01:06.935 --> 00:01:08.552
this is a wide table.

11
00:01:08.552 --> 00:01:11.140
Each new month would need a new column.

12
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.353
I name the table Payroll and click OK.

13
00:01:56.353 --> 00:01:57.680
Now let's reshape it.

14
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:29.571
Select the Name column, then on the Transform tab, choose Unpivot Other Columns.

15
00:02:29.571 --> 00:02:32.280
Everything except Name turns into rows.

16
00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.733
Now we have three columns:

17
00:02:52.733 --> 00:02:54.360
Name, Attribute, and Value.

18
00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.020
Each person appears three times, once for each month.

19
00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.680
This long shape is perfect for charts and DAX.

20
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:19.000
I rename Attribute to Month, and Value to Salary, so the table is easy to read.

21
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.247
And to go back?

22
00:03:30.247 --> 00:03:32.567
Select Month, and choose Pivot Column.

23
00:03:32.567 --> 00:03:37.207
Pick Salary as the values column, and the months become columns again.

24
00:03:37.207 --> 00:03:39.140
Two clicks, two shapes. Brilliant!

